\title{Draft-Cost Dominance and Domain-Parameterised Depth\\ in CPU Speculative Decoding}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{30 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Speculative decoding accelerates autoregressive generation by having a small \emph{draft}
model propose $d$ continuation tokens which a large \emph{target} model verifies in a
single forward pass, committing the accepted prefix and leaving the output distribution
exactly unchanged. Its theory has been developed almost entirely in a regime---massively
parallel accelerators---where verification is effectively free and the draft's sequential
proposal cost is negligible. We study the opposite regime: target and draft both executing
on a general-purpose CPU, where the $d$ proposal steps are paid in full and verification
does not amortise.

We develop a block model in which throughput is a ratio of a \emph{yield} curve to a
\emph{cost} curve, and prove five families of results. First, an exact head-to-head
criterion and a \emph{cost-dominance law}: at large depth a draft is characterised by the
single invariant $c(1-a)$, relative per-token cost times rejection rate, so an acceptance
advantage must beat a cost disadvantage multiplicatively in the rejection rate. At measured
parameters from a twelve-cell experiment on a seven-billion-parameter target, the cheaper
draft---costing $11.8\%$ of a target token against the larger draft's $23.4\%$---wins all
six head-to-head cells, including the one where it accepts strictly less ($56.0\%$ versus
$60.3\%$). Second, a \emph{depth collapse} theorem: for every acceptance $a<1$ and cost
$c>0$ there is a depth past which speculation is a net loss, with explicit gate
$(1-a)(1+cd)>1$. Third, a \emph{unimodality} theorem: for any yield with non-increasing
increments over an affine cost, throughput is unimodal in depth, so greedy hill-climbing is
exact; this yields a canonical \emph{stopping depth} that is globally optimal and monotone
in acceptance. Fourth, an \emph{averaging law} for position-resolved acceptance: the
reported mean acceptance of any fixed non-increasing survival profile is automatically
non-increasing in depth, so measured acceptance decay is uninformative about drafter
degradation; the law nevertheless supplies a falsifiable realisability test, which the
measured data pass, together with explicit survival profiles reproducing all six measured
acceptance percentages exactly. Fifth, a \emph{convex hardware cost curve}, calibrated on
three cells, predicting all twelve measured speedups within $11\%$ relative error;
convexity is forced, since no affine cost reproduces the calibration cells.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} speculative decoding, draft models, throughput optimisation,
survival profiles, unimodality, convex cost curves, CPU inference.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The mechanism}

An autoregressive language model generates token $t_{n+1}$ by a full forward pass
conditioned on $t_1,\dots,t_n$. The computation is inherently sequential and, for a large
model, expensive per token.

\emph{Speculative decoding} breaks the sequentiality by gambling. A cheap draft model
generates $d$ candidate continuations sequentially. The target then evaluates all $d+1$
positions in one forward pass---possible because, given the candidate string, all
conditioning contexts are known---and accepts the longest prefix consistent with its own
decoding rule. On the first rejection the target's own token is emitted and the suffix is
discarded. Under greedy decoding the output is identical to what the target would have
produced alone; under sampling, a correction preserves the target's distribution exactly.
Speculative decoding is a pure latency optimisation, never an approximation.

The gamble has a price: the $d$ draft steps are performed whether or not their outputs
survive. If drafting is cheap relative to verification the price is negligible; if drafting
is expensive, or if verification of a wide batch itself costs more than verification of a
single position, the price bites.

\subsection{The regime studied here}

Reported practice sits overwhelmingly on GPUs, where a batch of $d+1$ positions is verified
in essentially the time of one, and where two heuristics circulate: prefer the largest
affordable draft, because acceptance dominates; and increase depth as long as acceptance
holds up.

We study CPU-only inference: a four-bit-quantised seven-billion-parameter instruction-tuned
target, eight threads on a desktop-class processor, greedy decoding, baseline throughput
$5.79$ tokens per second. Two same-family drafts of $0.5$B and $1.5$B parameters, with
measured relative per-token costs $c \approx 0.118$ and $c \approx 0.234$
target-token-equivalents. Two domains---English prose and source code---with prompts of
roughly $500$ tokens. Three depths $d \in \{2,4,8\}$; twelve configurations, eight runs
each.

