Three features demand explanation, and each becomes a theorem below.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The cheap draft wins everywhere}, including code at $d=8$, where the
expensive draft accepts strictly more ($60.3\%$ against $56.0\%$) and is nonetheless $23\%$
slower. There is no crossover.
\item \textbf{Deep speculation is a net loss for prose} ($0.979$, $0.982$ at $d=8$) but the
best configuration for code ($1.661$). Optimal depth is domain-dependent.
\item \textbf{Reported acceptance decays with depth} in both domains, faster for prose.
Whether this reflects drafter degradation is exactly what the averaging law of
Section~\ref{sec:profiles} settles---negatively.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The block model}

Time is measured in units of one target decode step, so plain decoding produces one token
per unit time and all speedups are dimensionless.

\begin{definition}[Block cost]
For relative per-token draft cost $c \ge 0$ and depth $d \in \Nat$,
\[ \cost(c,d) = 1 + c\,d, \]
one verification pass plus $d$ sequential draft steps. Note $\cost(c,0)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Geometric yield]
If each drafted position is accepted independently with probability $a \in [0,1]$, the
expected tokens committed per block is
\[ \Ygeo(a,d) = \sum_{i=0}^{d} a^i, \]
the free correction token plus the expected accepted prefix length.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Throughput]
The speedup over plain decoding is
\[ \sigma(a,c,d) = \frac{\Ygeo(a,d)}{\cost(c,d)}, \qquad \sigma(a,c,0)=1 .\]
\end{definition}

Both $\cost(c,d)>0$ (for $c \ge 0$) and $\Ygeo(a,d)>0$ (for $a\ge 0$), so comparisons may
always be cross-multiplied.

\begin{lemma}[Exact comparison criterion]\label{lem:cmp}
For $c,c' \ge 0$ and depths $d,e$,
\[ \sigma(a,c,d) < \sigma(a',c',e)
\iff \Ygeo(a,d)\,\cost(c',e) < \Ygeo(a',e)\,\cost(c,d). \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Cross-multiplication by the two positive denominators.
\end{proof}

Since $(1-a)\Ygeo(a,d) = 1-a^{d+1}$ by telescoping, for $0 \le a < 1$ we have the uniform
bound $\Ygeo(a,d) \le (1-a)^{-1}$ for all $d$: \emph{the yield is bounded by the reciprocal
of the rejection rate, while the cost is unbounded.} Everything in Sections 3 and 4 follows
from this asymmetry.

\section{Draft-cost dominance}

\begin{theorem}[Cheap-draft law at equal acceptance]
Let $a \ge 0$, $0 \le c < c'$ and $d \ge 1$. Then $\sigma(a,c',d) < \sigma(a,c,d)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:cmp} the claim reduces to $\cost(c,d) < \cost(c',d)$, true since $d\ge1$
and $c<c'$, the yield being strictly positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cost dominance, all six head-to-heads]\label{thm:six}
With $c_{\mathrm{small}}=0.118$, $c_{\mathrm{large}}=0.234$ and the measured acceptances,
the small draft is strictly faster in every cell:
\begin{align*}
\sigma(0.632,0.234,2) &< \sigma(0.639,0.118,2), &
\sigma(0.834,0.234,2) &< \sigma(0.716,0.118,2), \\
\sigma(0.519,0.234,4) &< \sigma(0.477,0.118,4), &
\sigma(0.748,0.234,4) &< \sigma(0.630,0.118,4), \\
\sigma(0.449,0.234,8) &< \sigma(0.309,0.118,8), &
\sigma(0.603,0.234,8) &< \sigma(0.560,0.118,8).
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each line is a rational inequality: apply Lemma~\ref{lem:cmp} and evaluate the two finite
geometric sums.
\end{proof}

In five of the six cells the large draft accepts strictly more---in one case by nearly
twelve percentage points---and still loses. The hypothesised crossover at high acceptance
does not occur at the measured operating points.

\begin{theorem}[Deep-draft limit]\label{thm:limit}
For $0 \le a < 1$ and $c>0$,
\[ \lim_{d\to\infty} d\cdot\sigma(a,c,d) = \frac{1}{c\,(1-a)} . \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $d\,\sigma(a,c,d) = \Ygeo(a,d)\cdot \frac{d}{1+cd}$. The first factor converges to
$(1-a)^{-1}$, the second to $c^{-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic cost dominance]
Let drafts $A,B$ have parameters $(a_A,c_A)$, $(a_B,c_B)$ with $a_A,a_B \in [0,1)$,
$c_A,c_B>0$. If $c_A(1-a_A) < c_B(1-a_B)$ then there is $D$ with
$\sigma(a_B,c_B,d) < \sigma(a_A,c_A,d)$ for all $d \ge D$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The hypothesis orders the two limits of Theorem~\ref{thm:limit} strictly, since inversion
is strictly decreasing on the positive reals; eventual ordering of $d\,\sigma$ follows, and
dividing by $d>0$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

At depth, drafts are ranked by the scalar $c(1-a)$: relative cost times rejection rate. An
acceptance gain enters only through $1-a$, a comparatively insensitive lever near
$a \approx 0.6$. For the measured pair at code depth $8$:
$0.118 \times 0.440 = 0.0519$ against $0.234 \times 0.397 = 0.0929$.

\begin{corollary}[Required crossover acceptance]
With $c_A=0.118$, $a_A=0.560$, $c_B=0.234$, draft $B$ attains an invariant at most that of
$A$ only if $a_B \ge 0.778$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$0.234(1-a_B) \le 0.05192$ gives $1-a_B \le 0.2219$.
\end{proof}

The large draft measured $60.3\%$ at that cell: it needs a further $17.5$ points merely to
reach parity.

\section{Depth: collapse, unimodality, and the canonical selector}

\begin{theorem}[Depth gate]\label{thm:gate}
Let $0 \le a<1$, $c \ge 0$. If $(1-a)\cost(c,d) > 1$ then $\sigma(a,c,d)<1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The hypothesis says $(1-a)^{-1} < \cost(c,d)$; with $\Ygeo(a,d) \le (1-a)^{-1}$ this gives
$\Ygeo(a,d) < \cost(c,d)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Depth collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
For every $a\in[0,1)$ and $c>0$ there is $D$ with $\sigma(a,c,d)<1$ for all $d \ge D$;
explicitly any $D > a/((1-a)c)$ works.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $d > a/((1-a)c)$ we get $a < (1-a)cd$, hence $1 < (1-a)(1+cd)$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:gate}.
\end{proof}

Instantiated, the model reproduces all four measured depth-$8$ and depth-$4$ signs: prose
at $d=8$ is a predicted loss for both drafts, code at $d=8$ a win for the small draft, and
prose at $d=4$ a win for the small draft.

\subsection{Unimodality}

\begin{definition}
For arbitrary yield $Y:\Nat\to\mathbb{R}$ and cost $c \ge 0$ write
$\Sigma(Y,c,d) = Y(d)/\cost(c,d)$. Call $Y$ \emph{concave} if its increments
$Y(d+1)-Y(d)$ are non-increasing in $d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Marginal form]\label{lem:marginal}
For $c \ge 0$,
\[ \Sigma(Y,c,d+1) < \Sigma(Y,c,d) \iff \bigl(Y(d+1)-Y(d)\bigr)\cost(c,d) < c\,Y(d). \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Cross-multiply, using $\cost(c,d+1) = \cost(c,d)+c$, and simplify.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Decline propagates]\label{thm:decline}
Let $c \ge 0$ and let $Y$ be non-negative, non-decreasing and concave. If
$\Sigma(Y,c,d+1) < \Sigma(Y,c,d)$ then $\Sigma(Y,c,d+2) < \Sigma(Y,c,d+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:marginal} the hypothesis is
$(Y(d+1)-Y(d))\cost(c,d) < c\,Y(d)$. Passing to $d+1$, the increment on the left is no
larger (concavity) while $Y$ and $\cost$ both grow, so the inequality is preserved.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unimodality; exactness of greedy tuning]\label{thm:greedy}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:decline}, suppose $\Sigma(Y,c,k) \le
\Sigma(Y,c,k+1)$ for all $k<D$ and $\Sigma(Y,c,D+1) < \Sigma(Y,c,D)$. Then
$\Sigma(Y,c,d) \le \Sigma(Y,c,D)$ for \emph{all} $d\in\Nat$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Below $D$ chain the improvements; above $D$ iterate Theorem~\ref{thm:decline}, so $\Sigma$
is non-increasing on $[D,\infty)$.
\end{proof}

The geometric yield is concave, its increments being $a^{d+1}$, so the theorem applies to
$\sigma$ verbatim: the grid $\{2,4,8\}$ cannot have missed a second hump, and the measured
prose collapse is terminal.

By contrast an \emph{affine} yield has no interior optimum: writing
$\Sigma_{\mathrm{mean}}(q,k,d) = (1+qd)/(1+kd)$, this is non-decreasing in $d$ when
$k \le q$ and non-increasing when $q \le k$. Strict concavity of the yield is therefore
\emph{necessary} for the observed pattern of a win at $d=4$ and a loss at $d=8$ within one
configuration.

\subsection{Monotonicity of the frontier}

\begin{theorem}[Frontier monotonicity]\label{thm:frontier}
Let $0<a\le a'$, $c\ge0$, $d<e$. If $\sigma(a,c,d) \le \sigma(a,c,e)$ then
$\sigma(a',c,d) \le \sigma(a',c,e)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put the comparison in the equivalent ``deepening pays'' form
$T(a,d,e)\cost(c,d) \ge c\,(e-d)\,\Ygeo(a,d)$ with $T(a,d,e)=\sum_{i=d+1}^{e}a^i$. The key
inequality is $T(a,d,e)\Ygeo(a',d) \le T(a',d,e)\Ygeo(a,d)$ for $0\le a\le a'$, which holds
term by term: each product $a^i a'^{\,j}$ with $i>d\ge j$ is matched by $a'^{\,i}a^{j}$,
and $a^i a'^{\,j} \le a'^{\,i}a^{j}$ since $i>j$ and $a \le a'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monotone stopping]\label{cor:stop}
If deepening from $D$ to $D+1$ fails to pay at acceptance $a'$, it also fails at any
smaller $a \in (0,a']$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The canonical stopping depth}

\begin{definition}
For $a\in[0,1)$ and $c>0$,
\[ D^\star(a,c) = \min\{D\in\Nat : \sigma(a,c,D+1) < \sigma(a,c,D)\}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Well-definedness]
The set above is non-empty.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise $\sigma(a,c,\cdot)$ would be non-decreasing, hence $\ge \sigma(a,c,0)=1$
everywhere, contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}.
\end{proof}

Unimodality alone does not produce a stopping depth---a curve increasing forever has
none---so non-emptiness is precisely the formal trace of the fact that on a CPU sequential
drafting is never asymptotically free.

\begin{theorem}[Global optimality]
$\sigma(a,c,d) \le \sigma(a,c,D^\star(a,c))$ for all $d\in\Nat$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By minimality no decline occurs below $D^\star$ and one occurs at $D^\star$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The depth law]
For $0<a\le a'<1$ and $c>0$, $D^\star(a,c) \le D^\star(a',c)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At $D=D^\star(a',c)$ deepening fails at $a'$; by Corollary~\ref{cor:stop} it fails at $a$,
so $D$ lies in the stopping set of $a$.
\end{proof}

A domain that accepts more should draft at least as deep---always.

\begin{theorem}[The measured split]
At the small-draft cost $c=0.118$,
\[ D^\star(0.477,0.118)=2, \qquad D^\star(0.630,0.118)=3, \]
each a global optimum over all depths. In particular
$\sigma(0.477,0.118,3)<\sigma(0.477,0.118,2)$ while
$\sigma(0.630,0.118,2)<\sigma(0.630,0.118,3)$: at one and the same decision the two domains
disagree, so no static depth is optimal for both.
\end{theorem}

The endpoints are genuinely excluded: at $a=0$ the drafter is useless and the selector
returns $0$; at $a=1$ it is perfect and the stopping set is empty.

\section{Position-resolved acceptance: survival profiles}\label{sec:profiles}

\subsection{Why the independent model must be abandoned}

\begin{theorem}[Falsification of the independent reading]
For every $a \in [0,0.8]$,
\[ \sigma(a,0.118,8) < \sigma(a,0.118,4). \]
For contrast, $\sigma(0.85,0.118,4) < \sigma(0.85,0.118,8)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At $a=0$ the yield is constant while cost grows. For $0<a\le0.8$: if depth $8$ were at
least as good as depth $4$ at $a$, Theorem~\ref{thm:frontier} would transport this to
$a=0.8$, where the corresponding polynomial inequality is false by direct evaluation.
\end{proof}

The measured code cells have depth $8$ strictly faster than depth $4$ ($1.661$ versus
$1.616$) at a reported acceptance of $56.0\%$. Hence the reported percentage is
\emph{not} a per-position independent acceptance probability.

\subsection{Survival profiles and the averaging law}

\begin{definition}[Survival profile]
A \emph{survival profile} is $S:\Nat\to\mathbb{R}$ with $S(0)=1$ and $S(k+1)\le S(k)$,
interpreted as the probability that the first $k$ drafted positions are all accepted. The
block yield is $Y_S(d) = \sum_{k=0}^{d} S(k)$. The independent model is $S(k)=a^k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Reported acceptance]
The harness reports the committed fraction of drafted tokens,
\[ A_S(d) = \frac{Y_S(d)-1}{d} = \frac1d \sum_{k=1}^{d} S(k), \]
the arithmetic mean of the drafted positions' survival probabilities.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Averaging law]\label{thm:avg}
For every survival profile $S$ and all $1 \le d \le e$, $A_S(e) \le A_S(d)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Treat $e=d+1$ and induct. Monotonicity gives $S(k) \ge S(d+1)$ for $1\le k\le d$, hence
$Y_S(d)-1 \ge d\,S(d+1)$, so
\[ A_S(d+1) = \frac{(Y_S(d)-1)+S(d+1)}{d+1}
\le \frac{(Y_S(d)-1)\bigl(1+\tfrac1d\bigr)}{d+1} = \frac{Y_S(d)-1}{d} = A_S(d). \qedhere \]
\end{proof}

Consequently the headline pattern---prose $63.9 \to 47.7 \to 30.9$, code
$71.6 \to 63.0 \to 56.0$---carries \emph{no} information about whether the drafter degrades
with depth. Any fixed profile, measured at increasing depths, yields a decaying reported
acceptance.

\subsection{A falsifiable necessary condition}

\begin{theorem}[Block-mean test]
For any survival profile $S$ and $d<e$,
\[ (e-d)\bigl(Y_S(d)-1\bigr) \ge d\bigl(Y_S(e)-Y_S(d)\bigr), \]
i.e.\ the mean survival over positions $d+1,\dots,e$ is at most the mean over $1,\dots,d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left sum has $d$ terms each $\ge S(d)$; the right has $e-d$ terms each $\le S(d)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
No survival profile satisfies $A_S(2)=0.50$ and $A_S(4)=0.70$: reported acceptance rising
with depth is unrealisable.
\end{corollary}

The measured data pass. As block means: code $0.716,\ 0.544,\ 0.490$; prose
$0.639,\ 0.315,\ 0.141$---both non-increasing.

\subsection{Exact reconstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Realisability of the measured acceptances]
There exist survival profiles with
$A_{S_{\mathrm{code}}}(2,4,8) = (0.716,\,0.630,\,0.560)$ and
$A_{S_{\mathrm{prose}}}(2,4,8) = (0.639,\,0.477,\,0.309)$, namely
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
$k$ & $0$ & $1$ & $2$ & $3$ & $4$ & $\ge 5$ \\
\midrule
$S_{\mathrm{code}}(k)$  & $1.000$ & $0.800$ & $0.632$ & $0.560$ & $0.528$ & $0.490$ \\
$S_{\mathrm{prose}}(k)$ & $1.000$ & $0.700$ & $0.578$ & $0.350$ & $0.280$ & $0.141$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both tables are non-increasing with $S(0)=1$; the six means are direct evaluations of the
partial sums.
\end{proof}

All six reported percentages are therefore consistent with a single depth-independent
per-position structure per domain. The mechanism of the domain split becomes visible: the
code profile decays gently across positions $2\to3$ ($0.632\to0.560$) while the prose
profile falls off a cliff ($0.578\to0.350$). By Theorem~\ref{thm:avg} no reported mean
could ever have revealed this; only position-resolved instrumentation can.

\begin{remark}[Honest limitation]
Only three cumulative sums per domain are pinned by the data. The reconstruction is
\emph{not} unique, and the results are stated as realisability plus a falsifiable necessary
condition---never as identification of the true profile.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The local optimality rule}

\begin{theorem}[Marginal-survival stopping rule]
For any $S$ and $c \ge 0$, writing $\Sigma_S(d) = Y_S(d)/\cost(c,d)$,
\[ \Sigma_S(d) < \Sigma_S(d+1) \iff c\cdot\Sigma_S(d) < S(d+1). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:marginal} with $Y=Y_S$ and $Y_S(d+1)-Y_S(d)=S(d+1)$, then divide by the
positive $\cost(c,d)$.
\end{proof}

Deepen while the next position's survival probability exceeds the marginal cost times the
throughput already attained. Since $Y_S$ is automatically concave for monotone $S$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy} applies, so the local rule may be applied greedily and still
returns the global optimum: no grid search, no backtracking, one scalar comparison per
depth.

\begin{theorem}[The prescription, derived]
With the reconstructed profiles and marginal per-position cost $k=0.287$---the average
marginal cost over depths $4$ to $8$ of the hardware curve of Section~\ref{sec:cost}---
\[ \Sigma_{S_{\mathrm{prose}}}(5) < \Sigma_{S_{\mathrm{prose}}}(4), \qquad
\Sigma_{S_{\mathrm{code}}}(4) < \Sigma_{S_{\mathrm{code}}}(8). \]
Prose stops paying at depth $4$; code still gains from $4$ to $8$.
\end{theorem}

The deployed rule ``$d=8$ for code, $d=4$ for prose'' is thus a theorem about the
reconstructed profiles and one shared cost parameter, not a fitted observation.

\section{The hardware cost curve}\label{sec:cost}

\begin{theorem}[Affine cost is falsified]
There are no reals $b,k$ with
\[ 1+0.716\cdot2 = 1.352\,(b+2k),\quad 1+0.630\cdot4 = 1.616\,(b+4k),\quad
   1+0.560\cdot8 = 1.661\,(b+8k). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The three equations are linear in $(b,k)$ and the $3\times2$ system is inconsistent:
eliminating $b$ pairwise gives contradictory values of $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[The fitted CPU block cost]
\[ C(\mathrm{extra},d) = 1.5401 + (0.0992+\mathrm{extra})\,d + 0.0151\,d^2, \]
with $\mathrm{extra}=0$ for the small draft and $\mathrm{extra}=0.116$ for the large one
(exactly the measured difference $0.234-0.118$). The three coefficients are fixed by the
three code / small-draft cells alone. The predicted speedup of a cell with reported
acceptance $q$ is $\hat\sigma(q,\mathrm{extra},d) = (1+qd)/C(\mathrm{extra},d)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Fixed per-block overhead]
$C(\mathrm{extra},0) = 1.5401 > 1$: a block costs strictly more than one verification pass
even before any drafting.
\end{proposition}

This is why the shallow $d=2$ cells underperform the naive affine model: there is a real
fixed cost per speculative round trip.

\begin{proposition}[Anti-amortisation]
$C$ is strictly convex in depth: for every $d$,
$C(\mathrm{extra},d+1)-C(\mathrm{extra},d) < C(\mathrm{extra},d+2)-C(\mathrm{extra},d+1)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The second difference is the constant $2\times0.0151>0$.
\end{proof}

This inverts the GPU picture: on a saturated CPU widening the verification batch spills
working sets out of cache and lengthens the pass.

\begin{theorem}[Verification-overhead bracket]
Suppose a marginal per-position cost $k \ge 0$ reproduces both measured depth-$8$ signs
under the mean-yield reading: $(1+0.309\cdot8)/(1+8k)<1$ and $(1+0.560\cdot8)/(1+8k)>1$.
Then $0.309 < k < 0.560$; since the small draft costs only $0.118$ per token, verification
must charge $w = k-0.118 \in (0.191,\,0.442)$ target-steps per extra position.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each inequality is linear in $k$ after clearing the positive denominator.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Out-of-sample validity]
With $C$ calibrated on the three code / small-draft cells only, every one of the twelve
measured speedups satisfies
$|\hat\sigma - \sigma_{\mathrm{meas}}| \le 0.11\,\sigma_{\mathrm{meas}}$, using nothing but
that cell's measured acceptance. Nine of the twelve are out-of-sample; the worst relative
error is $10.6\%$.
\end{theorem}

