The separation this establishes is the organising claim of the paper: cost is universal
across domains, yield is domain-specific. One hardware curve, calibrated in one domain,
predicts the other. The residuals are, honestly, systematic rather than random---large-draft
cells over-predicted at shallow depth, prose cells at deep depth---suggesting that the
extra draft cost is itself depth-dependent, as the larger draft's own key-value cache grows
across the block.

\section{An algorithmic summary}

\paragraph{(A) Draft selection.} Rank candidate drafts by $c(1-a)$, smallest first;
justified exactly at large depth and, at the measured operating points, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:six}. Cost: one measurement of $c$ and $a$ per candidate.

\paragraph{(B) Depth tuning.} From $d=0$, compare $\Sigma(d+1)$ with $\Sigma(d)$ and stop at
the first non-improvement. The result is the global optimum $D^\star$, and it moves
monotonically with acceptance. Cost: $O(D^\star)$ comparisons, no backtracking; in local
form each comparison needs only the next position's survival probability and the current
throughput, both estimable online.

\paragraph{(C) Realisability audit.} Given reported acceptances at increasing depths, check
that the block means are non-increasing. Failure indicates a measurement or bookkeeping
error, since no survival profile can produce rising block means. Passing, reconstruct an
explicit profile by differencing the cumulative sums and drive procedure (B) with it.

\section{Discussion}

Three pieces of received practice fail in the CPU regime. \emph{``Prefer the larger draft;
acceptance dominates''} is false: the ranking invariant is $c(1-a)$, and the measured
$1.5$B draft would need $77.8\%$ acceptance merely to reach parity at the decisive cell,
against a measured $60.3\%$. \emph{``Deeper is better as long as acceptance holds up''} is
false: depth collapse is unconditional, and bites at $d=8$ for prose on the measured
hardware. \emph{``Falling acceptance with depth means the drafter degrades''} is not even
wrong: it is an arithmetic identity for any fixed drafter.

What does transfer is the block model itself; the CPU enters only through the numerical
size of $c$ and the convexity of the cost curve.

\paragraph{Practical prescription.} A $0.6$-gigabyte four-bit draft delivers up to $66\%$
additional throughput on a seven-billion-parameter target with no accelerator and
bit-identical output. Use the smallest competent draft, quantised aggressively; set $d=8$
for code and $d=4$ for prose; do not use a static depth, which forfeits better than $25\%$
of throughput on one side of the split or the other.

\paragraph{Limitations.} One model family, one machine, greedy decoding, prompts of about
$500$ tokens, twelve configurations at eight runs each. Reported acceptance is the overall
drafted-token fraction, not a per-position curve. Absolute throughput figures are specific
to the machine's memory configuration; only within-round ratios should be compared. The
reconstructed profiles are realisability witnesses, not identifications, and the cost curve
has three parameters fitted on three cells and validated on nine more at an $11\%$
tolerance with visibly systematic residuals. None of these bound the general theorems,
which hold for arbitrary parameters.

\section{Future work}

\paragraph{Curvature invariance across draft sizes.} The analysis separates a
domain-specific yield from a hardware-specific cost, and the cost curve's curvature came
out an order of magnitude below its slope---small, but provably nonzero, since no affine
cost fits the calibration cells. If curvature is a property of the target's verification
pass rather than of the draft, it must be identical for both drafts, with only the linear
term moving. The residual pattern of the twelve-cell test is systematic in exactly the way
a draft-dependent curvature would produce, so a single extra run at $d\in\{3,6\}$ for both
drafts discriminates the two explanations.

\paragraph{Position-resolved acceptance and the prose cliff.} The reconstructed prose
profile falls from $0.578$ to $0.350$ between positions $2$ and $3$ while the code profile
decays from $0.632$ to $0.560$, and the averaging law proves that no reported mean can
reveal this. Conjecture: the prose cliff sits at the first \emph{content} token after a
syntactically forced continuation, so its position is a function of the prompt's syntax
rather than of the depth. Exporting the per-position accept/reject vector instead of its
mean turns the existence statements into identification.

\paragraph{A deployable monotone depth controller.} Unimodality makes hill-climbing exact
and the stopping depth monotone in acceptance. A runtime controller that estimates the
current survival probability and applies the marginal-survival rule should track the
optimal depth without oscillation, adapting per request---and, if the cliff conjecture
holds, per syntactic position within a request.

\paragraph{Further axes.} A key-value-cache quantisation ladder on the same target;
transfer of the weight-quantisation floor; and transfer of the depth knee-law to other
model scales.

\section{Conclusion}

Throughput is yield over cost. Yield is a domain-specific survival curve whose reported
summary statistic is provably uninformative about its shape. Cost is a convex hardware
curve with a fixed per-block overhead and a marginal charge far above the draft's own
cost---verification anti-amortises. The optimal depth is the first position at which
next-position survival falls below marginal cost times current throughput; it is a global
optimum reachable by pure hill-climbing, and it rises monotonically with acceptance, which
is why code drafts deeper than prose and could not have done otherwise. Drafts are ranked
at depth by cost times rejection rate, which is why the cheaper, worse-guessing model wins
every measured head-to-head. Every feature of the twelve measured cells---including the two
that contradict accelerator folklore---is a corollary.

